text:
Yes. In my snake demo the whole board is a flat vector, and the network is a pure "one step" function. The loop that feeds the output back in each tick lives outside the network, in plain JS.

The state vector has 593 numbers:

- `life[196]`: for each of the 14×14 cells, how many more ticks it stays part of the body (0 = empty);
- `head[196]`: one-hot position of the head;
- `food[196]`: one-hot position of the food;
- `len`: snake length;
- `dir[4]`: one-hot direction for this tick (from the keyboard or autopilot).

The network is a pure step function: give it this tick’s board and direction, and it returns next tick’s board. It has no memory.

A small plain-JS loop does everything else each tick: reads the key, calls the network, writes the result back as the new board, places food when the network reports “ate”, and resets on a crash. All the game rules live inside the network, built only from weighted sums and switches: moving, growing, eating, collisions.
